**********Source Check**********

The claim 'The concept of the *Gesamtkunstwerk* can be applied in a certain way.' is supported by the citation.
<accept> The author discusses how he "fervently believed that architecture could sponsor a reunification of the arts" and how "vestiges of this have remained" in his work, such as the drawings presented. In the comments, the author further clarifies that "the key thing in these drawings is the ‘integration’—you can’t really separate what is architecture, sculpture, and painting," explicitly linking his work to the concept of *Gesamtkunstwerk* in a specific way. </accept>

**********Source Check**********

The claim 'A desire for a total work of art with something existed despite differences and dissimilarities resulting from the variability of various conditions and contexts.' is NOT supported by the citation.
<reject> The text mentions the historical existence of the idea of Gesamtkunstwerk and its manifestations in various historical and cultural contexts (Egyptian, Greek, Medieval, Renaissance, Baroque). It also states that at the beginning of the modern age, there was a belief that new forms of integrating the arts were needed "as had been done in the great cultures of the past." While this implies a desire for a total work of art across different conditions, it does not explicitly state that this desire *existed despite differences and dissimilarities resulting from the variability of various conditions and contexts*. The text focuses on how the idea was realized or believed in, rather than emphasizing its persistence *despite* inherent dissimilarities. The closest mention is LW's personal belief which had to work "very much against all tendencies and trends," but this is a specific, individual belief, not a general statement about a desire that existed historically despite differences.</reject>

